    {
      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To solve this problem, I'll first find the perimeter of the original triangle and then use the scale factor to determine the lengths of the sides of the similar triangle.\nThe perimeter P of a triangle with side lengths a, b, and c is given by P = a + b + c. So for the original triangle with side lengths 6 cm, 7 cm, and 9 cm, its perimeter is P = 6 cm + 7 cm + 9 cm = 22 cm.\nSimilar triangles have proportional side lengths, and the ratio of the perimeters is equal to the ratio of the corresponding side lengths. So if the perimeter of the similar triangle is 110 cm, the ratio of its perimeter to the original triangle's perimeter is 110 cm / 22 cm = 5 / 1.\nThis means that each side of the similar triangle is 5 times longer than the corresponding side of the original triangle. Specifically, the longest side of the similar triangle is 9 cm * 5 = 45 cm. \nTherefore, the length of the longest side of the similar triangle is 45 cm.",
